\section*{GFF limits of weighted integer Lipschitz surfaces}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Weighted triangular integer Lipschitz heights are claimed to have a Dirichlet Gaussian field limit for every fixed edge-change weight $x\in[1/\sqrt2,1]$.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Result 1.} Let $D$ be bounded and simply connected with $C^2$ Jordan boundary. Approximate it from inside by regions bounded by simple honeycomb cycles, containing every enclosed vertex and edge, with uniformly converging boundary parametrizations and eventual containment of every compact subset of $D$. On enclosed faces, sample integer heights with boundary value zero and $|h(u)-h(v)|\le1$ for adjacent faces, using weight
\[
 \mathbb P_{\delta,x}(h)=Z_{\delta,x}^{-1}x^{N_\delta(h)},\qquad
 N_\delta(h)=\sum_{\{u,v\}:u\sim v}\mathbf1_{\{h(u)\ne h(v)\}}.
\]
Each unordered adjacency is counted once. Extend $h_\delta$ constantly over faces and by zero elsewhere, so $h_\delta(f)=\int_Dh_\delta f$. For each fixed $x\in[1/\sqrt2,1]$, there is a domain-independent $\sigma(x)>0$ such that
\[
 h_\delta/\sigma(x)\Longrightarrow\Phi_D\quad\text{in }\mathcal D'(D),
\]
where the zero-Dirichlet GFF has covariance $(-\Delta_D)^{-1}$. All finite joint test-function distributions and mixed moments converge.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Conditional comparisons across exact cuts and shell-count estimates bound smeared moments. Reflected plane normalization fixes the variance, while separated-pin transfer and boundary-attachment estimates remove boundary errors and identify Gaussian moments, including the critical endpoint.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Weighted triangular integer Lipschitz heights are claimed to have a Dirichlet Gaussian field limit for every fixed edge-change weight $x\in[1/\sqrt2,1]$. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

